  "body": "Consider `xn--xn---epa`. It appears that using the current **domain to Unicode** algorithm (as implemented by Node.js), this would get converted to `xn--é`. But applying **domain to ASCII** on `xn--é` would produce a Punycode decoding failure. It sounds like domain to Unicode (or even UTS 46) should return failure on such labels.\r\n\r\nThis is somewhat important because Firefox can create such double-encoded labels:\r\n\r\n```js\r\n» new URL('http://xn--é').hostname\r\n❮ \"xn--xn---epa\"\r\n```",
